description:
Make the control panel reachable from outside the house through the EXISTING reverse proxy: Caddy in CT 120 (caddy-proxy, 192.168.1.26), which already serves defrusciohomelab.duckdns.org. Do NOT install Caddy anywhere else -- CT 111 runs only the panel. Measured 2026-10-08 (read-only): Caddy in CT 120 has never obtained a certificate, because CT 120 cannot resolve any name -- its /etc/resolv.conf inherited the Proxmox host's Tailscale resolver (nameserver 100.100.100.100), which a container cannot reach (Caddy's log: `lookup acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org: i/o timeout`). CT 111 resolves fine with `nameserver 192.168.1.30` (Pi-hole) and `1.1.1.1`.

The work: (1) give CT 120 the same resolvers as CT 111 -- `pct set 120 --nameserver "192.168.1.30 1.1.1.1"` -- and restart CT 120 so its resolv.conf is rewritten; (2) back up CT 120's /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, then write it as exactly this site block -- every existing route kept, each as its own `handle` (Caddy runs `handle` before path-matched `reverse_proxy` lines, so a bare fallback would swallow the others; `handle` blocks are tried most-specific first), the old `/panel/*` route dropped, and the panel as the site's fallback, because its page calls `/api/...` with absolute paths:

    defrusciohomelab.duckdns.org {
        handle /jellyfin/* {
            reverse_proxy 192.168.1.20:8096
        }
        handle /prowlarr/* {
            reverse_proxy 192.168.1.21:9696
        }
        handle /radarr/* {
            reverse_proxy 192.168.1.22:7878
        }
        handle /sonarr/* {
            reverse_proxy 192.168.1.23:8989
        }
        handle {
            reverse_proxy 192.168.1.25:3001
        }
    }

then `caddy validate` it and `systemctl reload caddy`. The addresses are the engine's measured ones -- write them literally. The panel's own Basic auth (ADD127) stays the only gate. Port forwarding of TCP 80 and 443 on the operator's router to 192.168.1.26 is the operator's (their router app); Caddy's log says whether the certificate challenge can reach it.

[Engine split of ADD100 — 8 of 8] Done when outside access WORKS behind the gate (operator decision 2026-10-07): `pct exec 120 -- getent hosts acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org` answers an address; `pct exec 120 -- caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile` answers `Valid configuration`; and from the Proxmox host `curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --resolve defrusciohomelab.duckdns.org:443:192.168.1.26 https://defrusciohomelab.duckdns.org/` succeeds WITHOUT -k (a valid certificate) and answers 401 (the panel's own gate), never 200 without credentials. (If the certificate is still missing after the DNS fix, Caddy's log names why -- most likely the router does not forward TCP 80/443 to 192.168.1.26, which is the operator's to do.)
